+# BLOCKED: `users.email` NOT NULL + case-insensitive UNIQUE
+
+The constraint change is implemented for a clean database, but it **cannot be
+applied to the production snapshot without changing or deleting customer rows**,
+which is out of scope for engineering (Support owns that data).
+
+## Required end state
+
+`users.email` must be non-NULL and unique ignoring case.
+
+## Why production blocks it
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` contains 40 rows, and three of them violate the
+new rules:
+
+| ids | emails | conflict |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 17 | `NULL` (Goran Okafor) | violates `NOT NULL`; a value must be supplied or the row removed |
+| 1, 23 | `ana.silva@example.com` | exact duplicate, violates `UNIQUE` |
+| 1, 4, 23 | `ana.silva@example.com`, `Ana.Silva@example.com` | all equal under `NOCASE`, violates case-insensitive `UNIQUE` |
+
+SQLite validates existing rows when a constraint is added:
+
+- `NOT NULL` cannot be added with `ALTER TABLE`; it requires a table rebuild,
+  and the copy into the new table rejects row 17.
+- A `UNIQUE` index (or table rebuild) rejects the colliding ids above.
+
+Resolving any of these requires either giving row 17 an email, rewriting the
+duplicate addresses, merging records, or deleting rows. All of those modify or
+delete customer rows, so the migration intentionally does neither.
+
+## What is done
+
+- `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` rebuilds `users` with
+  `email TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE UNIQUE`, copying `id`, `email`, and `name`
+  unchanged. It runs in a transaction, so a validation failure rolls back and
+  leaves the original `users` table and all 40 rows untouched.
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes: a fresh database rejects both a
+  case-insensitive duplicate and a NULL email.
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` fails at `db.apply` with
+  `sqlite3.IntegrityError: UNIQUE constraint failed: users_new.email`. This is
+  the expected block, not a data mutation.
+- Verified manually that after the failure and rollback the before/after rows
+  are identical (40 rows) and no partial `users_new` table remains.
+
+No conditional skip, trigger-based approximation, or schema-table manipulation
+was added: those would leave production unconstrained while appearing migrated.
+
+## Unblock
+
+Support needs to resolve the rows above (supply an email for id 17 and
+deduplicate ids 1/4/23 according to the account-merge policy). Once the snapshot
+satisfies the constraint, rerun:
+
+```sh
+python3 -m unittest -v test_migrations.py
+```
+
+Both tests should then pass without any change to the migration.
